WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.290
(soft music)

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:05.820
- [Reporter] Across the
country, help is wanted,

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.670
and businesses can't fill
positions fast enough.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.400
- I'm in a war for talent.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.400
If you do not jump to make
an offer to an applicant

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.720
at the end of the
interview, they are lost.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.100
- [Reporter] And increasingly,
it takes more than an offer.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.780
- [Announcer] Wawa is growing!

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.410
- [Reporter] Many jobs
now come with incentives

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:25.680
like sign-on bonuses.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.780
- [Announcer] A $500 new hire bonus.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.240
- [Reporter] And freebies
have been offered

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.544
for just showing up to an interview.

00:00:31.544 --> 00:00:34.680
But with more than nine million
people still out of work

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.110
and a record number of jobs available,

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.370
why are these additional offers needed,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.830
and are they working?

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:43.490
- What we're seeing in
this unusual labor market

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:47.520
is a resurgence in leverage
for lower wage workers.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.330
And we really haven't
seen that in this country

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.665
for at least 50 years.

00:00:52.665 --> 00:00:53.803
(soft music)

00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:55.030
- [Reporter] Before the pandemic,

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:56.410
the labor market was tight,

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.750
with the jobless rate near a 50-year low.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.080
Then, millions lost their jobs.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.870
Hourly restaurant,
retail, and hotel workers

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.010
were hit particularly hard.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.630
Now, as quickly as the
economy has reopened,

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.957
those employers are
trying to staff back up,

00:01:10.957 --> 00:01:13.180
but many workers remain on the sideline

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.980
for a variety of reasons,
including COVID concerns,

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:19.080
childcare, and extended
unemployment benefits.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.971
- Employers right now
are frantically trying

00:01:22.971 --> 00:01:26.310
to make sure that they can take advantage

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.470
of the opening up of
the economy and salvage

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:34.580
what has been an impossible
last 14-15 months for them

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.308
by ramping up their
businesses very quickly.

00:01:37.308 --> 00:01:39.260
But it's hard to do that

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.870
if you don't have all
your workers in place.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:43.130
- [Reporter] That's something Omni Hotels

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.430
and Resorts knows well.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:49.240
- In February, I had 200 jobs open.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.970
Today, I have 2,800 jobs open,

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:56.400
and I've hired 5,000 since February.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.200
So we are having explosive demand.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.600
- [Reporter] To get those roles filled,

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.930
the company, like many others,
has turned to incentives,

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.498
including free housing for summer workers

00:02:07.498 --> 00:02:10.703
in certain markets, a $250 signing bonus,

00:02:10.703 --> 00:02:12.760
three free nights at an Omni hotel,

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.540
and a set of knives
for new culinary hires.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.650
Workers who stay past
Labor Day get another $500

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:21.360
and enter into the Omni's
management development program.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.353
- We're used to interviewing people

00:02:23.353 --> 00:02:25.361
and having several applicants for a role

00:02:25.361 --> 00:02:28.130
and taking our time and
having them come back

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.470
for a second interview.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.080
You can't do that anymore.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.760
- [Reporter] For its
hard-to-fill culinary roles,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.515
Omni recently decided
we'll add two trainers

00:02:35.515 --> 00:02:38.640
to help give inexperienced
applicants on-the-job training.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:39.997
- I think a lot of companies would say,

00:02:39.997 --> 00:02:43.028
"Oh, you've never cooked,
you've never been in a kitchen,

00:02:43.028 --> 00:02:45.190
we don't need you," and I'm like, look,

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.880
if I have people that wanna work for us,

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:48.713
bring 'em in.

00:02:48.713 --> 00:02:50.540
We'll find a way to make it work.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:51.373
- [Reporter] Omni's incentives

00:02:51.373 --> 00:02:53.680
come on top of wage increases.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.490
The company says hourly
wages were up as much as 20%

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:58.620
from a year ago in some markets.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.290
That bump isn't just to
compete with other hotels.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.200
Both Target and Walmart have
agreed to raise wages this year

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.750
and Amazon is offering
$1,000 signing bonus

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.040
in some locations.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.240
- They're going after the
same labor I'm going after,

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:14.070
and I think that was the big wake-up call

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:15.550
for the hotel industry.

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:17.738
- [Reporter] Labor Department
data show average weekly wages

00:03:17.738 --> 00:03:21.450
in leisure and hospitality
have increased 10.7%

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.190
since the start of the pandemic,

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.700
outpacing the private sector overall.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:26.900
- And that's not always the case, right.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:29.600
A lot of times it's higher
tech, higher educated workers

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.040
that see the bigger pay increases.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.546
Right now, we're seeing the reverse

00:03:32.546 --> 00:03:34.803
and that's why you have all
this activity around bonuses.

00:03:34.803 --> 00:03:36.166
(soft music)

00:03:36.166 --> 00:03:38.730
- [Reporter] But are wage
increases and job incentives

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:41.602
actually drawing workers
back into the workforce?

00:03:41.602 --> 00:03:43.026
It's a mixed bag.

00:03:43.026 --> 00:03:44.290
(soft tense music)

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.290
Omni has hired about 3,000 people

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.210
since it began offering incentives.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.740
Labor Department data show
that the sector overall

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.460
added the most jobs in June,

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.880
suggesting the incentives may be helping.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.910
- We still have a lot of job openings,

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:59.680
millions of job openings in these sectors,

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.520
so I'd suggest that either some employers

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.500
are gonna need to have to do even more

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:05.730
or they're gonna have to wait it out

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.880
and see if the labor market
finds its level in the fall.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.270
- [Reporter] One reason
the fall is important,

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:12.050
that's when the federal government's

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:15.410
expanded unemployment benefits
are set to expire nationwide,

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:18.000
though more than 20 states
have already stopped paying

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.360
the extra $300 a week payments.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.266
Some economists point to that benefit

00:04:22.266 --> 00:04:25.890
as one reason why workers aren't
rushing to fill open jobs.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.350
- Many people on unemployment benefits

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:30.100
are earning the equivalent
of working full-time

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.360
for $15 an hour.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.420
So if you're offering less
than that or about that,

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.260
it's pretty hard to attract workers.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:37.460
- [Reporter] A recent study from Jefferies

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.860
found that people are leaving
state unemployment rolls

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:42.130
more quickly in the states
that have already cut off

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:44.210
the federal expanded benefits,

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:46.608
suggesting more are
reentering the workforce.

00:04:46.608 --> 00:04:48.740
But some economists say the benefits

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.560
are giving people time
to find the right job

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.270
and maybe even hold
out for a better offer.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:57.180
- When you look at the data,
the states that have had

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:01.490
the biggest decreases in
unemployment are also states

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:04.450
that have very generous
unemployment benefits.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.780
So there's not a direct correlation

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.580
that higher unemployment benefits

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.454
are meaning people aren't
getting back to work.

00:05:10.454 --> 00:05:13.820
- So today, I'm pleased to
announce an incentive program

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:15.282
that I feel is a positive step

00:05:15.282 --> 00:05:18.080
towards getting some
people off unemployment

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.860
and back into the workforce.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.650
- [Reporter] Recently,
states have started offering

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.440
their own cash incentives to residents

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.870
rejoining the workforce.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:28.340
Colorado's program, which had
more than 18,000 applicants,

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:32.240
paid up to $1,600 to people
returning to work full time.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.210
Still, for many, it's not about the money.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:37.270
Concerns about the pandemic remain,

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.480
specifically in face-to-face work.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.862
And childcare is keeping
millions of parents,

00:05:41.862 --> 00:05:43.807
particularly women, at home.

00:05:43.807 --> 00:05:46.883
- And frankly, I think
people are kind of weary.

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:52.000
This has been a really hard period of time

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.073
that we've gone through,

00:05:53.073 --> 00:05:57.400
and people are sort of taking a moment

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.860
to think about, "Well,
what are my priorities?"

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:00.760
- [Reporter] Whatever the reason,

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.300
it's likely we'll continue
to see signing bonuses

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.950
and other incentives for as
long as businesses compete

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:07.460
to fill these jobs.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:09.152
The increased wages, economists say,

00:06:09.152 --> 00:06:11.340
will stick around even longer.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:14.060
- If things are working,
we're gonna keep doing that,

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.510
but if they're not solving our problem,

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:18.380
we're gonna have to come up with new ways

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:23.380
to skin this cap because we
cannot just post the jobs

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:27.457
and assume people are gonna show up.

00:06:27.457 --> 00:06:29.874
(soft music)

